# sentence_id = 102
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = Socrates: for I am ashamed, both for your sake and for ours, your friends, lest this whole affair of yours be thought to have been handled with a kind of cowardice on our part — the entry of the case into court, how it was brought in when it could have been avoided, the very conduct of the trial, how that went, and then this last absurd thing of all, as it were, as if some wretched cowardice of ours let the chance slip away, since we did not save you, nor did you save yourself, though it was possible and feasible, if we had been of even the slightest use.
# literal_translation = Socrates: as I at least, both on your behalf and on behalf of us your friends, am ashamed lest the whole affair concerning you be thought to have been done with some cowardice of ours — the entrance of the suit into the court, how it entered when it was possible not to enter, and the very contest of the suit itself, how it came about, and this, the last thing indeed, this here, as it were a laughingstock of the affair, [coming about] through some wretchedness and through our cowardice, [it] seeming that we did not save you, nor did you yourself save yourself, though it was possible and feasible, if there was even some small use from us.
