# sentence_id = 301
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = Or are you so wise that you have failed to notice that one's homeland is more to be honored than one's mother and father and all one's other ancestors, and more revered and more sacred, and held in higher regard both among gods and among men of sense, and that one must revere it and yield to it and humor it when it is angry even more than a father, and either persuade it or do whatever it commands, and submit to suffering, if it orders one to suffer anything, in silence—whether that means being beaten or imprisoned, or, if it leads one to war to be wounded or killed, one must do these things, and this is what justice requires—and that one must not give way or retreat or abandon one's post, but both in war and in court and everywhere else one must do whatever the city and one's homeland commands, or else persuade it by what is naturally just?
# literal_translation = Or are you thus wise that it has escaped you that [your] fatherland is more honorable than [your] mother and father and all [your] other ancestors, and more august and more sacred, and in greater esteem both among gods and among men who have sense, and [that] one must revere [it] and yield [to it] [0=rather/more] and flatter one's fatherland when it is angry [1=even more] than [one's] father, and either persuade [it] or do whatever it commands, and suffer [it], if it commands one to suffer anything, keeping quiet—whether [it commands] to be beaten or to be bound, whether [2=also] it leads [one] into war to be wounded or to be killed—these things must be done, and the just thus stands, and one must not give way nor retreat nor leave one's post, but both in war and in court and everywhere one must do what the city and [one's] fatherland commands, or persuade it by that by which the just is naturally [disposed].
