# sentence_id = 309
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = For we, having begotten you, raised you, educated you, and given you a share in all the good things we could, both to you and to all the other citizens alike, nevertheless proclaim in advance that it is open to any Athenian, once he has been examined and has seen how things stand in the city, and seen us, the laws, if we do not please him, to go away and take his own possessions wherever he wishes.
# literal_translation = For we, γάρ, you, having-begotten, having-reared, having-educated, having-given-a-share of-all-things of-which we-were able of-the-noble-things, to-you and to-the other all citizens, nevertheless we-proclaim-beforehand to-the one-having-made-the power, of-the Athenians, to-the one-wishing, whenever he-be-examined and he-see the-things in the city, the two matters, and us the laws, to-whom not we-may-please, we, [it]-is-permitted, having-taken the things of-himself, to-go-away, to-where he-may-wish.
