# sentence_id = 311
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = And whoever among you remains, seeing in what way we administer justice and manage the rest of the city's affairs, we say that he has now agreed with us in fact to do whatever we command; and we say that the one who does not obey does wrong in three ways — because he does not obey us who are, as it were, his parents, and because we are his nurturers, and because, having agreed to obey us, he neither obeys nor persuades us if we are doing something not right, even though we offer the alternative and do not harshly command him to do whatever we order, but allow him one of two things — either to persuade us or to do it — and he does neither of these.
# literal_translation = And whoever of you remains, seeing in what manner we both adjudicate the cases and administer the other things of the city, already we say this one to have agreed in deed with us to do these things which we might command; and we say the one not being persuaded does wrong in three ways, both because to us being his begetters he is not persuaded, and because to his nurturers, and because having agreed with us to suffer [these things], he neither is persuaded nor persuades us, if we are doing something not well, [and] we proposing and not harshly commanding to do what we bid, but allowing one of two things — either to persuade us or to do [it] — he does neither of these.
1	ὃς	ὅς	p	p3s---mn-	Case=n|Gender=m|Number=s|Person=3	5	SBJ	_	t_3853|gloss=whoever
