# sentence_id = 339
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = In the first place, if you go to one of the cities nearest by—Thebes or Megara, say—you will arrive as an enemy, Socrates, for both of them are well governed, and all who care about their own cities will look at you askance, thinking you a corrupter of the laws, and you will confirm for the jurors the impression that they judged the case correctly.
# literal_translation = And you yourself, first, on the one hand, if into one of the nearest cities you go, either to Thebes or to Megara—for both are well-governed—as an enemy you will come, O Socrates, to the constitution of these [cities], and as many as care for their own cities will look askance at you, considering [you] a corrupter of the laws, and you will confirm for the jurors the opinion [that they] judged the case rightly.
